package com.ankan.singleton;

public class Singleton {
    private int num = 0;
    private Singleton () { // private restricts from creating multiple objects

    }

    private static Singleton instance; // This is a static variable that will hold the single instance of the Singleton class. Being static ensures that the variable is shared across all instances of the class, but since the constructor is private, only the Singleton class itself can assign a value to it.

    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        // check whether 1 object only is created or not
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
/*
class Example {
    Singleton.instance = new Singleton();
}

SINCE INSTANCE IS PRIVATE, IT CAN'T BE ACCESSED OUTSIDE THE SINGLETON CLASS. SINCE SINGLETON CONSTRUCTOR IS PRIVATE
IT CANNOT CREATE A NEW OBJECT OUTSIDE THE SINGLETON CLASS.
*/

package com.ankan.singleton;

public class Singleton {
    private int internalNumber = 0;  // Example instance variable (not related to singleton pattern)

    // Private constructor prevents instantiation from outside the Singleton class
    // This is the key to the Singleton pattern - only Singleton can create its own instance
    private Singleton() {
        System.out.println("Singleton instance created (constructor called)");
        // Private constructor restricts creating multiple objects
        // No external class can use 'new Singleton()'
    }

    // Static variable that holds the single instance of the Singleton class
    // Being static ensures the variable belongs to the class itself, not to any instance
    // Since the constructor is private, only the Singleton class can assign a value to this variable
    private static Singleton uniqueInstance = null;

    // Static method that provides global access point to the single instance
    // Synchronized keyword makes it thread-safe (prevents multiple threads from creating multiple instances)
    public static synchronized Singleton getInstance() {
        // Check whether an instance has already been created or not
        // Lazy initialization: create instance only when first requested
        if (uniqueInstance == null) {
            uniqueInstance = new Singleton();  // Create the single instance
        }
        return uniqueInstance;  // Return the existing instance
    }

    // Optional: Method to display the instance's internal state
    public void displayInstanceInfo() {
        System.out.println("Singleton instance hashcode: " + this.hashCode());
        System.out.println("Internal number value: " + internalNumber);
    }

    // Optional: Method to modify internal state (demonstrates that single instance state is shared)
    public void setInternalNumber(int newValue) {
        this.internalNumber = newValue;
        System.out.println("Internal number updated to: " + internalNumber);
    }
}

/*
Important Notes about Singleton Pattern:
=========================================

1. Private Constructor:
   - Cannot be called from outside the Singleton class
   - Prevents external code from creating multiple instances
   - Example: Singleton obj = new Singleton(); // COMPILATION ERROR

2. Private Static Instance:
   - Holds the single instance of the class
   - Static means it belongs to the class level, not object level
   - Private means only Singleton class can access it directly

3. Public Static getInstance() Method:
   - Global access point to get the singleton instance
   - Static so can be called without an object: Singleton.getInstance()
   - Synchronized for thread safety in multi-threaded environments

4. Lazy Initialization:
   - Instance is created only when first requested, not at class loading time
   - Saves resources if the instance is never used

5. Thread Safety:
   - The 'synchronized' keyword ensures only one thread can execute getInstance() at a time
   - Prevents race conditions that could create multiple instances

What WON'T Work (External Access Attempts):
===========================================

class Example {
    void attemptToCreateSingleton() {
        // Cannot access private constructor:
        // Singleton invalidInstance = new Singleton();  // COMPILATION ERROR

        // Cannot access private static field:
        // Singleton.uniqueInstance = new Singleton();   // COMPILATION ERROR

        // Only valid way to get instance:
        Singleton validInstance = Singleton.getInstance();  // ✅ WORKS
    }
}

Since 'instance' is private, it cannot be accessed outside the Singleton class.
Since Singleton constructor is private, it cannot create a new object outside the Singleton class.
*/

